package scryer:runtime@1.0.0;

/// The typed, family-neutral runtime capabilities every plugin world imports
/// alongside `scryer:host/services@1.0.0`.
///
/// # Why a second import exists at all
///
/// `scryer:host/services` stays the single door for everything *encoded*: one
/// `host-call` carrying a postcard `PluginHostRequest`, so a new capability is
/// a new SDK enum variant and never a WIT revision. That shape cannot express
/// two things a guest needs from the runtime rather than from a service:
///
/// - **Concurrency.** `http` and `sleep` are `async func`s here, so a guest's
///   `process` export can await them while the host drives other work and
///   still observe cancellation. An encoded `host-call` is a synchronous WIT
///   function by construction (see that package for why), so nothing reached
///   through it can yield.
/// - **Ambient facts with no request payload.** The monotonic and wall clocks,
///   the invocation deadline, the descriptor-bound configuration lookup, the
///   provider profile, plugin state and its compare-and-swap, and logging are
///   all cheap, typed, and called in loops by the plugin-owned pacing
///   primitives (start-rate gates, cooldowns, window quotas). Encoding each of
///   those as postcard round-trips would be pure overhead and would lose the
///   type-checked shapes below.
///
/// So the split is by *kind*, not by family: encoded and extensible on
/// `scryer:host/services`, typed and concurrent here. A family world imports
/// both and gets the whole surface.
///
/// # Why it is family-neutral
///
/// Every member of this interface is copied verbatim from
/// `scryer:indexer/host@1.1.0`, minus `emit-strategy-event` and its
/// `strategy-event-error` — the one pair that is genuinely indexer-only,
/// because it reports progress against a search plan that no other family has.
/// Everything that is left is what *any* family needs: an HTTP client, a
/// cancellable sleep, clocks, a deadline, config, a provider profile, state
/// with CAS, and a log sink.
///
/// Naming it `scryer:runtime` rather than reusing the indexer package matters
/// for a mechanical reason: a guest crate that merely *names* an import keeps
/// it alive in the component's type metadata, so shared plugin code (the PDK's
/// pacing primitives, the newznab engine) can only be linked into a subtitle
/// or download-client component if the import it names is one that component's
/// world actually declares. A neutral package is what lets one implementation
/// of that code serve every family.
///
/// # Migration
///
/// `scryer:indexer/host@1.1.0` is unchanged and keeps serving the indexer
/// world today; the two interfaces are structurally identical apart from the
/// strategy-event pair. The indexer world adopts `scryer:runtime/host` at its
/// next revision, at which point the duplicated definitions go away. Until
/// then a host serves both, which is exactly what the indexer host's own
/// 1.0/1.1 dual-contract support already does.
///
/// # Availability
///
/// Unlike `host-call`, these functions have typed failure channels of their
/// own: `http` reports `transport-error`, and everything else either returns an
/// `option` or cannot fail. A capability the host has not been configured with
/// is therefore an empty `option` or a `transport-error`, never a trap.
interface host {
  /// One HTTP header. Names are case-insensitive to the host, which normalizes
  /// them; a guest should not rely on the casing it sent surviving.
  record header {
    name: string,
    value: string,
  }

  record http-request {
    method: string,
    url: string,
    headers: list<header>,
    body: list<u8>,
  }

  record http-response {
    status: u16,
    headers: list<header>,
    body: list<u8>,
  }

  /// A failure of the HTTP transport itself.
  ///
  /// A response the plugin does not like — a 404, a 429, an error document —
  /// is a perfectly good `http-response`, not one of these. These mean no
  /// response exists.
  enum transport-error {
    invalid-request,
    forbidden-origin,
    timeout,
    cancelled,
    response-too-large,
    capacity,
    transport,
  }

  enum log-level {
    trace,
    debug,
    info,
    warn,
    error,
  }

  /// Perform one HTTP request through the host's client, subject to the
  /// plugin's allowed-host policy, its response cap, and the invocation
  /// deadline.
  http: async func(request: http-request) -> result<http-response, transport-error>;

  /// Sleep, cancellably. The host resolves this early when the invocation is
  /// cancelled, so a guest's pacing loop unwinds instead of holding a worker.
  sleep: async func(duration-ms: u64);

  /// A monotonic clock in milliseconds, on the host's timebase. Only
  /// differences are meaningful.
  monotonic-now-ms: func() -> u64;

  /// The monotonic instant this invocation must finish by, on the same
  /// timebase as `monotonic-now-ms`.
  operation-deadline-monotonic-ms: func() -> u64;

  /// Wall-clock milliseconds since the Unix epoch, for bucketing quotas that
  /// have to agree with a provider's own calendar day.
  wall-now-ms: func() -> u64;

  /// Read one descriptor-bound configuration value for this instance.
  config-get: func(key: string) -> option<string>;

  /// The encoded provider profile bound to this instance, when it has one.
  provider-profile: func() -> option<list<u8>>;

  /// Read one plugin-state value.
  state-get: func(key: string) -> option<list<u8>>;

  /// Atomically replace one plugin-state value if it currently equals
  /// `expected`, returning whether the swap happened.
  ///
  /// `none` means absent on either side, so this expresses insert-if-absent and
  /// delete-if-unchanged as well as replace. The host performs the comparison
  /// and the write under one lock: a guest must never emulate it with a
  /// `state-get` followed by a write.
  state-cas: func(key: string, expected: option<list<u8>>, replacement: option<list<u8>>) -> bool;

  /// Emit one log record into the host's tracing subscriber.
  log: func(level: log-level, message: string);
}
